WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.280
Same sun, same afternoon.

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:11.060
Why is the beach sand too hot to stand on, while the water beside it still feels

3
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:17.000
cool? The first film showed water molecules tugging on each other through hydrogen

4
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.540
bonds. Now we follow the sunshine's energy into that web.

5
00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:24.793
First, what does a thermometer read?

6
00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:30.493
Temperature tracks the average energy of motion of a substance's particles.

7
00:00:32.253 --> 00:00:36.593
Faster jiggling means a higher temperature, and slower jiggling means a lower one.

8
00:00:37.617 --> 00:00:41.357
Take equal masses of dry sand and water, side by side.

9
00:00:42.537 --> 00:00:44.897
Give each exactly the same amount of energy.

10
00:00:46.157 --> 00:00:47.877
Then watch where that energy goes.

11
00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:53.667
In the sand, the particles vibrate harder, and the pulls between them stretch a

12
00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:57.227
little. Its thermometer climbs about ten degrees.

13
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.880
In the water, the molecules speed up too.

14
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:07.480
But the web of hydrogen bonds also stretches, bends and rearranges, and that takes

15
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:14.080
extra energy. So each degree costs water more, and its thermometer climbs only about

16
00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:15.080
two degrees.

17
00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:20.510
This is specific heat, the energy needed to warm one gram by one degree.

18
00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:26.310
Per gram, water needs about five times as much energy as dry sand.

19
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:28.950
Its specific heat is high.

20
00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:31.950
Night falls, and both blocks cool.

21
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.850
Say each loses energy at the same steady rate.

22
00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:41.030
Will the water's temperature fall faster or slower than the sand's?

23
00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:42.930
Pause and decide.

24
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:56.070
Slower. Each degree of cooling must release about five times as much energy from the

25
00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:00.770
water. As its hydrogen bonds re-form, they give energy back.

26
00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:05.270
So the water stays warm longer, while the sand cools quickly.

27
00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.110
But a bay is not one gram.

28
00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:14.070
Heat capacity is the energy needed to warm a whole object by one degree.

29
00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.770
For one substance, it is the mass times the specific heat.

30
00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:23.430
A cup and a bathtub of water share the same specific heat.

31
00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.990
Yet the bathtub has far more heat capacity.

32
00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.990
Under the same heater, it warms far more slowly.

33
00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:33.473
Now picture the Bay.

34
00:02:35.173 --> 00:02:38.013
Waves and currents mix the warm surface water downward.

35
00:02:39.193 --> 00:02:42.273
So the day's sunshine spreads through a huge mass of water.

36
00:02:43.533 --> 00:02:47.273
Some energy also leaves the surface, as water evaporates.

37
00:02:48.233 --> 00:02:50.173
So three things keep a bay steady.

38
00:02:51.493 --> 00:02:55.193
Each gram is slow to warm, thanks largely to hydrogen bonds.

39
00:02:56.433 --> 00:02:58.593
There is a huge mass of mixing water.

40
00:02:59.853 --> 00:03:01.853
And evaporation carries some heat away.

41
00:03:02.933 --> 00:03:08.593
So fish, crabs and grasses in the Bay face gentler daily swings than the air above

42
00:03:08.593 --> 00:03:14.013
them. A buoy in the Bay measured both, and the lesson shows what it recorded.
